    <h2 style="margin-top:0">The honest pitch</h2>
    <p>I do one thing well, which is build custom-coded websites for small service businesses. I do not do paid ads, SEO consulting, content strategy, branding from scratch, copywriting at scale, or business coaching. The owners I serve usually need help with at least one of those things, and sometimes all of them.</p>
    <p>You probably do at least one of them, which means we have an obvious fit. I send the people I cannot help with strategy your way, and you send the people you cannot help with their actual website mine. The Partner Program is what formalizes that arrangement: a named relationship, tracked referrals, and recurring commission.</p>

    <h2>The terms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>20% commission for the first 12 months</strong> on every client you refer who signs the monthly plan ($175/mo). That works out to $35 per referred client per month, paid out on a consistent monthly cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>$400 one-time</strong> on every client you refer who signs the lump-sum plan ($4,000). Paid on project kickoff.</li>
      <li><strong>No cap.</strong> If you refer twenty clients in a year, you earn on twenty.</li>
      <li><strong>Tracked by named referral</strong>, not a cookie, not a UTM parameter that expires. The client says "X sent me," I credit you. Honor system, but I keep a public-to-you ledger of every referral and its status.</li>
      <li><strong>Paid via Stripe</strong> on a 1099 basis. Whatever your business form (LLC, S-corp, sole prop), I send a 1099-NEC at year end.</li>
      <li><strong>No exclusivity.</strong> You can refer to other developers too. I am not asking you to choose me; I am asking you to think of me when the right client comes up.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who this works for</h2>
    <p>The Partner Program is designed for service providers whose clients are small service businesses. Specifically:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Marketing agencies</strong> who run paid ads, SEO, or social for service businesses but do not build sites in-house. You probably already have a "we recommend our friend X" line; this makes it formal and pays you for it.</li>
      <li><strong>SEO consultants</strong> who audit small-business sites and routinely conclude "your site needs to be rebuilt before any of this SEO work matters."</li>
      <li><strong>Business coaches</strong> serving service-business owners who want a real website but do not know who to trust.</li>
      <li><strong>Accountants and bookkeepers</strong> who handle the money for small contractors and inspectors and routinely hear "I need a better website but I do not know what to do."</li>
      <li><strong>Industry-association members</strong> (NRCA, NAHI, NACHI, NEC, BIA, etc.) who interact with service-business owners professionally and trust them to send work back.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It does not work for: lead-gen platforms that resell leads (Angi, HomeAdvisor, Thumbtack), website-flipping services, or companies that already build sites and would be a direct competitor.</p>

    <h2>What I deliver to your client</h2>
    <p>So you know what you are referring to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Custom-coded site, no WordPress, no AI templates. <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">Six live demo sites</a> show the range.</li>
      <li>$0 design deposit on the monthly plan. The client pays $175/mo with nothing up front; the build starts immediately.</li>
      <li>2&ndash;3 weeks from discovery call to launch.</li>
      <li>95&ndash;100 Google PageSpeed on every site.</li>
      <li>Hosting, SSL, security, unlimited content updates included on the monthly plan.</li>
      <li>Direct support, the client emails me, not a queue.</li>
      <li>WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility baseline.</li>
      <li>Local-business schema and on-page SEO foundation that pairs cleanly with whatever ongoing SEO work you do.</li>
      <li>Code ownership transfer (lump-sum) or static export (monthly) at any time. The client is never locked in.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to refer</h2>
    <p>Three options, in order of how I prefer them:</p>
    <ol style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.85rem;list-style:decimal inside;margin:1rem 0">
      <li><strong>Warm intro by email.</strong> Best conversion rate. CC me with a one-line "X meet Jon, Jon meet X" intro. I take it from there. Send to <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}" class="inline-link">{{ site.email }}</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Tell the client to mention you.</strong> They book a discovery call at <a href="/contact/" class="inline-link">/contact/</a> and write your name in the "tell me about your business" field. I credit you on the call.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the partner-referral form.</strong> Fill in your details + the client's contact info on the <a href="/refer/" class="inline-link">referral form</a> with the topic set to "partner referral." I reach out to the client mentioning you sent me.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Onboarding</h2>
    <p>If this sounds like a fit, the onboarding is:</p>
    <ol style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.85rem;list-style:decimal inside;margin:1rem 0">
      <li>Email me at <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}" class="inline-link">{{ site.email }}</a> with subject "Partner Program."</li>
      <li>Twenty-minute call to make sure your client base is the right shape and I am the right developer for them.</li>
      <li>Sign a one-page partner agreement (similar in shape to the <a href="/agreement/" class="inline-link">service agreement</a>: plain English, public terms).</li>
      <li>Add yourself as a referrer in my CRM. From here on, every referred client is tagged to you.</li>
      <li>Quarterly partner check-in: I send you a short report on referrals received, signed, and your year-to-date commission.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Why 20% / 12 months? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">It is meaningful money for you and sustainable for me. 20% of $175 is $35; over 12 months on a successful referral you earn $420. Twenty referrals a year is $8,400 of recurring commission to you. After 12 months the commission ends so the math works for me long-term.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if a client cancels in month 4? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">You earned commission for months 1&ndash;3 and you keep what you earned. Month 4 onward stops because the client is no longer paying. No clawbacks.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Can I be both a client and a partner? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes. Several agencies build their own site with me and refer clients to me. Each role has its own paperwork.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Do you have a co-marketing component? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Once you have referred 3+ clients who signed, I will list you on this site as a recommended partner (with your permission). I will write about you on the blog if there is a topic worth covering. Co-marketing is a relationship, not a transaction; it builds organically.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if a client mentions a different referrer than the one who sent them? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">I credit who the client says. If you are confident you sent a client and they credited someone else, email me, I will check the email trail. Disputes are rare and resolved by reading the actual emails.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
